{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PnArVFcpPnt27X8N1BbEMQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've gathered enough information about CoRL review guidelines. Let me also try to find the specific rating scale used by CoRL. Based on my knowledge and search results, CoRL typically uses a 1-10 scale similar to other ML conferences on OpenReview. Let me try one more search to confirm.\n\nActually, from CoRL 2024 statistics on Paper Copilot, I recall the typical CoRL review form uses:\n- Overall score: 1-10 scale\n- Confidence: 1-5 scale\n\nThe CoRL acceptance rate is typically around 30-35%.\n\nLet me now proceed with the review. I have:\n\n\n- The paper itself (8 pages plus references)\n- The review context with imagined experiments and expected results\n- CoRL reviewer guidelines covering originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance, and limitations\n\nNow I'm setting up two specialized reviewers: one focused on methods and experiments, another on novelty and related work. I'll run them in parallel and then conduct a structured roundtable discussion where they independently evaluate first, share their assessments, converge on key points, and synthesize a final recommendation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2813,"cache_read_input_tokens":62170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2813},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"ee7e0eff-7504-498b-b1aa-026209c4bb7a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PnArVFcpPnt27X8N1BbEMQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper, review context, and CoRL guidelines. CoRL uses a 1-10 overall rating scale on OpenReview (acceptance rate ~30-35%). Now dispatching two parallel reviewers: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2813,"cache_read_input_tokens":62170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2813},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"3d5fb883-e804-4469-b238-19310a1922a7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PnArVFcpPnt27X8N1BbEMQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U6BBV3mx4ktHfYbR5GdLQQ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=strong accept, 1=strong reject). Acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n- Criteria: originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance to robot learning, limitations.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Multivariate Conformal Prediction Help Robot Action Correction? A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\nThis paper presents ACR-VLA, a pipeline applying multivariate conformal prediction (CP) to VLA action correction. The system extracts 45D structured conformal features from K=10 flow-matching samples via Conformal Geometry Score (CGS), uses Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) with Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) for online calibration, and feeds features into a learned Conformal Residual Module (CRM) for action correction. Through 11 experiments on LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus, the paper finds all three main claims fail pre-registered kill criteria, reporting systematic negative results with detailed failure analysis.\n\n## Key Claims Tested (all fail):\n1. Per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction (A3 ablation: 0.8pp gap, p=0.38)\n2. COC provides reliable self-referential calibration (Spearman ρ=0.18 with task success)\n3. Inference-time correction stacks orthogonally on training-time methods (+1.1% on SRPO, p=0.29)\n\n## Key Tables/Results:\n- Table 1: Full pipeline: SmolVLA base 95.0%/28.3% (nom/Plus-Avg) → ACR-VLA 91.0%/31.3%. Only +3pp on Plus but -4.2pp on nominal.\n- Table 2: Covariance diagnostics: MVP 3D shrinkage=0.42, effective rank=2.7 vs VLA 7D shrinkage=0.91, effective rank=1.3.\n- Table 3: A3 kill test: removing all 45D conformal features → 30.5% vs 31.3%, gap=0.8pp, p=0.38 (not significant).\n- Table 5: A4: Per-DOF (45D) 31.3% vs Scalar (1D) 31.0% vs None (0D) 30.5% — all indistinguishable.\n- Table 6: A5: CGS vs Bonferroni: 31.3% vs 31.1%, Δ=0.2pp.\n- Table 7: A7: CGS epistemic 31.3% vs DiU aleatoric 31.6% vs Combined 52D 31.4% vs None 30.5%.\n- Table 8: Feature importance: observation gradient 0.041 vs conformal features ~0.002-0.004.\n- Table 9: COC signal: ρ=0.18, ellipsoid narrowing 6.5% (<10% threshold).\n- Table 11: A1 (no ACI) 30.8%, A2 (no CRM) 29.5%, Full 31.3%.\n- Table 12: A6: COC 31.3%/6.5% narrowing vs Episode success 31.8%/12.8% narrowing.\n- Table 13: SRPO stacking: 82.1% → 83.2%, +1.1pp, p=0.29.\n- Table 14: Per-perturbation: only camera (+5.2) and noise (+4.8) significant; layout (-0.5) and language (-1.2) show negative transfer.\n- Table 15: SnapFlow: 7.9x speedup but sample diversity drops to 35%, performance -2.1pp.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe review context lists 13 imagined experiments. The paper covers all of them across its 11-experiment structure (some are combined). The paper reports a full pipeline eval, 7 ablations (A1-A7), per-perturbation breakdown, feature importance analysis, and SnapFlow integration.\n\n## Your Review Task\nAssess:\n1. **Experimental design quality**: Are the 11 experiments well-designed? Are controls appropriate? Are the kill criteria reasonable?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are paired t-tests across task sets appropriate? Are the sample sizes (3 seeds, 50 tasks) sufficient? Are the p-values and effect sizes interpreted correctly?\n3. **Methodology**: Is the CGS/ACI/CRM pipeline well-specified? Is Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage the right choice? Is the covariance degeneracy analysis sound?\n4. **Completeness**: Does the ablation suite adequately decompose the pipeline? Are there missing experiments?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is enough detail provided?\n6. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**.\n\nProduce your review in this format:\n\n### R1 Review: Methods &amp; Experiments\n\n**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths** (numbered list)\n\n**Weaknesses** (numbered list)\n\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n**Missing Experiments or Analyses**\n\n**Minor Issues**\n\n**Scores:**\n- Soundness: X/4\n- Empirical Methodology: X/4\n- Reproducibility: X/4\n- Overall (methods/experiments perspective): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2813,"cache_read_input_tokens":62170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2813},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"fd7a982b-ee27-4869-afb2-536813eaad5f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ac4e6e5c0a1586144","tool_use_id":"toolu_01U6BBV3mx4ktHfYbR5GdLQQ","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=strong accept, 1=strong reject). Acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n- Criteria: originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance to robot learning, limitations.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Multivariate Conformal Prediction Help Robot Action Correction? A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\nThis paper presents ACR-VLA, a pipeline applying multivariate conformal prediction (CP) to VLA action correction. The system extracts 45D structured conformal features from K=10 flow-matching samples via Conformal Geometry Score (CGS), uses Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) with Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) for online calibration, and feeds features into a learned Conformal Residual Module (CRM) for action correction. Through 11 experiments on LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus, the paper finds all three main claims fail pre-registered kill criteria, reporting systematic negative results with detailed failure analysis.\n\n## Key Claims Tested (all fail):\n1. Per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction (A3 ablation: 0.8pp gap, p=0.38)\n2. COC provides reliable self-referential calibration (Spearman ρ=0.18 with task success)\n3. Inference-time correction stacks orthogonally on training-time methods (+1.1% on SRPO, p=0.29)\n\n## Key Tables/Results:\n- Table 1: Full pipeline: SmolVLA base 95.0%/28.3% (nom/Plus-Avg) → ACR-VLA 91.0%/31.3%. Only +3pp on Plus but -4.2pp on nominal.\n- Table 2: Covariance diagnostics: MVP 3D shrinkage=0.42, effective rank=2.7 vs VLA 7D shrinkage=0.91, effective rank=1.3.\n- Table 3: A3 kill test: removing all 45D conformal features → 30.5% vs 31.3%, gap=0.8pp, p=0.38 (not significant).\n- Table 5: A4: Per-DOF (45D) 31.3% vs Scalar (1D) 31.0% vs None (0D) 30.5% — all indistinguishable.\n- Table 6: A5: CGS vs Bonferroni: 31.3% vs 31.1%, Δ=0.2pp.\n- Table 7: A7: CGS epistemic 31.3% vs DiU aleatoric 31.6% vs Combined 52D 31.4% vs None 30.5%.\n- Table 8: Feature importance: observation gradient 0.041 vs conformal features ~0.002-0.004.\n- Table 9: COC signal: ρ=0.18, ellipsoid narrowing 6.5% (<10% threshold).\n- Table 11: A1 (no ACI) 30.8%, A2 (no CRM) 29.5%, Full 31.3%.\n- Table 12: A6: COC 31.3%/6.5% narrowing vs Episode success 31.8%/12.8% narrowing.\n- Table 13: SRPO stacking: 82.1% → 83.2%, +1.1pp, p=0.29.\n- Table 14: Per-perturbation: only camera (+5.2) and noise (+4.8) significant; layout (-0.5) and language (-1.2) show negative transfer.\n- Table 15: SnapFlow: 7.9x speedup but sample diversity drops to 35%, performance -2.1pp.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe review context lists 13 imagined experiments. The paper covers all of them across its 11-experiment structure (some are combined). The paper reports a full pipeline eval, 7 ablations (A1-A7), per-perturbation breakdown, feature importance analysis, and SnapFlow integration.\n\n## Your Review Task\nAssess:\n1. **Experimental design quality**: Are the 11 experiments well-designed? Are controls appropriate? Are the kill criteria reasonable?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are paired t-tests across task sets appropriate? Are the sample sizes (3 seeds, 50 tasks) sufficient? Are the p-values and effect sizes interpreted correctly?\n3. **Methodology**: Is the CGS/ACI/CRM pipeline well-specified? Is Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage the right choice? Is the covariance degeneracy analysis sound?\n4. **Completeness**: Does the ablation suite adequately decompose the pipeline? Are there missing experiments?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is enough detail provided?\n6. **Weaknesses in methods/experiments**.\n\nProduce your review in this format:\n\n### R1 Review: Methods &amp; Experiments\n\n**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths** (numbered list)\n\n**Weaknesses** (numbered list)\n\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n**Missing Experiments or Analyses**\n\n**Minor Issues**\n\n**Scores:**\n- Soundness: X/4\n- Empirical Methodology: X/4\n- Reproducibility: X/4\n- Overall (methods/experiments perspective): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n","uuid":"8dbcc6ce-af79-4aef-8533-74284b548cf0","session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4"}
