{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lu3RuQmrXSDUGHzNYhVGpp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260513T023849\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 5/10 — Borderline Reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-13T03:12:12.046337+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: ACR-VLA\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: ACR-VLA: Inference-Time Dimension-Selective Action Correction via Multivariate Conformal Prediction for Vision-Language-Action Models\n11\t**Target Venue**: CoRL 2026\n12\t**Review Mode**: Full (2-reviewer roundtable)\n13\t**Dream ID**: 20260513T023849\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Venue Calibration\n18\t\n19\t**CoRL** evaluates on: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations. Historical acceptance rate ~25–35%. Rating scale 1–10 (10=top 5% seminal, 8=clear accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below). CoRL places high value on robotics relevance, empirical rigor, real-world applicability, and honest reporting of limitations.\n20\t\n21\t---\n22\t\n23\t## Roundtable Summary\n24\t\n25\t### Step 1: Independent Reviews\n26\t\n27\t**R1 (Methods + Experiments) — Score: 4/10**\n28\tFocus: methodological soundness, experimental rigor, statistical reporting.\n29\t\n30\t**R2 (Novelty + Related Work) — Score: 5/10**\n31\tFocus: novelty positioning, related work coverage, claim calibration.\n32\t\n33\t### Step 2: Cross-Sharing — Key Points Exchanged\n34\t\n35\tR1→R2: Flagged absence of statistical reporting (no CIs, no seed variance, no test methodology), no K sensitivity analysis despite ~15pp oracle-to-estimated degradation in MVP, and CRM training confound (augmentation memorization vs. genuine conformal utility). Also noted that all experimental figures referenced in text are absent from the 7-page PDF.\n36\t\n37\tR2→R1: Flagged that the 5-entry bibliography is a fatal deficiency (the text discusses 10+ methods by name without citations), the \"first to combine X with Y\" novelty framing is narrow, and the \"orthogonal gains\" claim (Claim 3) is tested on only one training-time method (SRPO). Also noted 45D feature dimensionality is arithmetically inconsistent in the text.\n38\t\n39\t### Step 3: Convergence\n40\t\n41\tBoth reviewers converge on the following assessment:\n42\t\n43\t**Points of Agreement:**\n44\t1. The ablation hierarchy (none 60.8% < scalar 66.8% < per-DOF 72.8%) is the paper's strongest evidence and is well-designed\n45\t2. The 5-reference bibliography is unacceptable at any venue — a fatal formatting deficiency\n46\t3. Single VLA backbone (SmolVLA) and single benchmark (LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus) limit generality claims\n47\t4. No experimental comparison with closest related methods (A2C2, ReconVLA, UPS, CP-in-the-Loop)\n48\t5. Honest per-perturbation breakdown and transparent language-perturbation limitation are commendable\n49\t6. The 45D dimensionality description contains internal inconsistencies\n50\t7. Statistical rigor is insufficient — no confidence intervals, error bars, or multi-seed reporting\n51\t8. Practical engineering merits (12ms overhead, SnapFlow compatibility, 131K params) are genuine strengths\n52\t\n53\t**Points of Divergence (Resolved):**\n54\t- R1 initially scored lower (4) due to concerns about results provenance; R2 (5) was more lenient on methodological concerns but harsher on novelty framing. After cross-sharing, both agree the score should reflect: strong empirical promise + significant presentation/rigor deficiencies. **Converged overall: 5/10 (Borderline Reject / Weak Reject).**\n55\t\n56\t---\n57\t\n58\t## Consolidated Review\n59\t\n60\t### 1. Summary\n61\t\n62\tACR-VLA proposes an inference-time correction module for flow-matching VLAs that extracts 45-dimensional per-DOF conformal statistics (CGS) from K=10 action samples with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, adapts conformal thresholds online via a novel Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) signal, and feeds these features into a lightweight 131K-parameter CRM for dimension-selective residual correction. On LIBERO-Plus, ACR-VLA improves base SmolVLA by +15pp and achieves 88.7% when stacked on SRPO (+6.6pp), establishing a new SOTA. Ablations demonstrate a clean gradient (none < scalar < per-DOF) and isolate per-DOF conformal features as the primary driver.\n63\t\n64\t### 2. Strengths\n65\t\n66\t**S1. Well-designed ablation hierarchy.** The A1–A4 ablations provide clean decomposition: A3 (no conformal features, 60.8%) < A4 (scalar, 66.8%) < Full (per-DOF, 72.8%). The 12pp gap between CRM-without-conformal and full ACR-VLA is the paper's most convincing evidence. This is complemented by the MVP point-reaching validation showing the same ranking in a controlled setting.\n67\t\n68\t**S2. Principled statistical methodology.** Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for the K=10, d=7 regime (p/n=0.7) is well-motivated and correctly addresses the ill-conditioning problem. The Mahalanobis-based nonconformity score capturing cross-dimensional correlations is theoretically sound.\n69\t\n70\t**S3. COC as self-referential signal is creative.** Exploiting temporal overlap structure of action chunks for coverage feedback addresses a real deployment constraint (no external reward). The 2.5× convergence speedup over episode-level supervision is practically meaningful.\n71\t\n72\t**S4. Epistemic vs. aleatoric distinction (A7) is well-argued.** CGS epistemic (72.8%) >> DiU aleatoric (63.9%), with the key insight that aleatoric uncertainty causes over-intervention on nominal data (−2.5pp). This is a genuine conceptual contribution.\n73\t\n74\t**S5. Honest limitation reporting.** Transparent disclosure that language perturbations yield no significant gain (p=0.31) and that gains correlate with CGS detectability is rare and appreciated. Per-perturbation breakdown with p-values (Table 4) raises the paper's credibility.\n75\t\n76\t**S6. Strong practical engineering.** 131K parameters, ~12ms overhead, compatibility with SnapFlow (4.9× speedup at −1.6pp), post-hoc deployment on frozen VLAs — all indicate a system designed for real use.\n77\t\n78\t**S7. Orthogonality evidence via SRPO stacking.** +6.6pp on top of an already-strong 82.1% baseline, with negligible nominal degradation (−0.3pp), is a meaningful result that supports the inference-time/training-time complementarity thesis.\n79\t\n80\t### 3. Weaknesses\n81\t\n82\t**W1 (Fatal). Bibliography contains only 5 entries.** The Related Work section discusses 10+ methods by name (A2C2, CP-in-the-Loop, UPS, DiU, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow, SnapFlow, etc.) without corresponding citations. This is unacceptable at any venue. The authors clearly know the literature — the textual discussion is accurate and specific — but the formal bibliography must include every referenced work. This alone would trigger a desk-reject concern at CoRL.\n83\t\n84\t**W2 (Major). No experimental comparison with closest baselines.** A2C2 (generic residual correction), ReconVLA (scalar CP-based sample selection), UPS (CP for strategy gating), and DiG-Flow (training-time augmentation) are all discussed in Related Work but absent from experimental tables. Ablation A3 approximates A2C2 but is not equivalent to running the actual method. Without head-to-head comparisons, the reader cannot determine whether ACR-VLA's gains come from its specific conformal feature design or from general properties of any inference-time correction applied to SmolVLA.\n85\t\n86\t**W3 (Major). No statistical reporting.** No confidence intervals, standard deviations, number of evaluation seeds, or test methodology descriptions appear anywhere. For CoRL, reporting mean ± std across ≥3 seeds is expected. The +15pp headline gain could be noisy if variance is high.\n87\t\n88\t**W4 (Major). Single VLA backbone and single benchmark.** All experiments use SmolVLA on LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus. The paper's language (\"any flow-matching VLA,\" \"orthogonal to training-time methods\") implies generality that is not empirically supported. Testing on at least one additional VLA (e.g., π0, OpenVLA) would substantially strengthen generality claims.\n89\t\n90\t**W5 (Major). \"Orthogonal gains\" claim (Claim 3) is under-supported.** Tested on only one training-time method (SRPO). Orthogonality is a stronger claim than additive improvement — it requires showing gains are preserved across multiple training-time approaches. No failure mode decomposition is provided to demonstrate that ACR-VLA and SRPO correct non-overlapping failures.\n91\t\n92\t**W6 (Moderate). No K sensitivity analysis.** K=10 is used throughout without ablation. The MVP experiment shows ~15pp degradation from oracle to K=10 estimated covariance. How does performance vary with K=5, K=15, K=20? This is essential for understanding the method's sensitivity and for practitioners choosing deployment configurations.\n93\t\n94\t**W7 (Moderate). CRM training confound.** CRM is trained on synthetically augmented data covering 5 perturbation types. The per-perturbation breakdown (Table 4) shows gains concentrated on augmentation-covered types (camera +19.5, noise +17.8) and minimal on uncovered types (language +2.1). A held-out perturbation type experiment (train CRM without one augmentation type, evaluate on it) would distinguish conformal feature utility from augmentation memorization.\n95\t\n96\t**W8 (Moderate). COC novelty may be overstated.** Temporal consistency checking is well-established in sequential prediction. The specific application to action chunk overlap is new, but the underlying principle is not. The COC-vs-episode-level ablation (A6) shows only 0.7pp final performance difference — COC's primary advantage is convergence speed, not final quality. This should be stated more precisely.\n97\t\n98\t**W9 (Moderate). 45D feature dimensionality is internally inconsistent.** Section 3.1 describes \"15-dimensional feature vector per DOF ... plus scalar volume, totaling 45 dimensions.\" But 15 features × 7 DOFs = 105, not 45. The actual construction (7 std + 7 conformal radii + 1 ellipsoid volume per action chunk timestep) needs arithmetic clarification.\n99\t\n100\t**W10 (Minor). \"Conformal\" framing may mislead.** Once CGS features pass through CRM, the corrected action carries no formal coverage guarantee. The paper should explicitly state that CP is used as a feature extractor, not as a guarantor of output quality. \"Conformal Refinement Module\" may create misleading expectations.\n101\t\n102\t**W11 (Minor). Missing figures.** The paper references an extensive figure set (Figs 2–14, S1–S5) in the experiment design, but the 7-page PDF contains only tables. Key visualizations — ellipsoid volume heatmaps, per-DOF correction magnitude comparisons, ACI convergence curves — would substantially strengthen the empirical narrative.\n103\t\n104\t### 4. Questions for Authors\n105\t\n106\t1. **Bibliography**: Is the 5-entry reference list a draft artifact? Will the camera-ready include full citations for all discussed methods?\n107\t\n108\t2. **Baseline comparisons**: Have you run A2C2 or ReconVLA on your setup? If A2C2 achieves above A3's 60.8%, that would narrow the claimed gap attributable to conformal features specifically.\n109\t\n110\t3. **Statistical significance**: How many seeds were used? What are the standard deviations on the headline numbers (72.8%, 88.7%)? Would the +15pp gain survive a paired bootstrap test?\n111\t\n112\t4. **K sensitivity**: How does performance degrade at K=5? How much does it improve at K=20? Is there a diminishing-returns curve?\n113\t\n114\t5. **Held-out perturbation**: If CRM is trained without camera augmentation, what is the gain on camera perturbation specifically? This isolates conformal feature utility from augmentation coverage.\n115\t\n116\t6. **Orthogonality breadth**: Have you tested ACR-VLA stacked on DiG-Flow or RobustVLA? If gains disappear for another training-time method, the \"orthogonal\" claim reduces to \"complementary to SRPO.\"\n117\t\n118\t7. **Feature importance implications**: Your gradient analysis shows covariance-related features contribute only ~21% (ellipsoid volume). Does this undermine the multivariate CP motivation? Would a simpler \"per-DOF std + scalar volume\" feature set achieve similar results without Mahalanobis computation?\n119\t\n120\t8. **Coverage guarantee**: Can you state any formal guarantee on the corrected action, or is CP purely a feature extractor? Would \"uncertainty-conditioned residual correction\" be a more accurate description than \"conformal refinement\"?\n121\t\n122\t### 5. Scores\n123\t\n124\t| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Converged | Rationale |\n125\t|-----------|----|----|-----------|-----------|\n126\t| **Originality** | 5 | 5 | **5** | Combination novelty (CP features → learned residual) is genuine but narrow. Individual components are established. COC is a creative instantiation of temporal consistency. |\n127\t| **Quality / Soundness** | 4 | 6 | **5** | Ablation design is strong; execution has gaps (no CIs, no K ablation, no head-to-head baselines, single VLA). |\n128\t| **Clarity** | 6 | 7 | **6** | Writing is clear and logical. 5-reference bibliography, 45D arithmetic error, and missing figures are significant presentation issues. |\n129\t| **Significance** | 5 | 6 | **5** | +15pp and 88.7% SOTA are meaningful if robust; single-benchmark evaluation, missing baselines, and no statistical reporting limit assessed significance. |\n130\t| **Relevance** | — | — | **8** | Highly relevant to CoRL: inference-time robustness for VLA deployment is a timely, practical problem. |\n131\t| **Overall** | 4 | 5 | **5** | Borderline Reject / Weak Reject |\n132\t| **Confidence** | 4/5 | 4/5 | **4/5** | |\n133\t\n134\t### 6. Meta-Review / Recommendation\n135\t\n136\t**Overall Assessment: 5/10 — Borderline Reject**\n137\t\n138\tACR-VLA addresses a well-identified gap (no prior method uses structured per-DOF conformal statistics as input features for learned action correction) with a technically sound pipeline and a clean ablation hierarchy that convincingly demonstrates per-DOF > scalar > none. The COC signal, epistemic-vs-aleatoric analysis, and SRPO stacking results are additional strengths. The practical engineering (12ms, 131K params, SnapFlow compatibility) is commendable.\n139\t\n140\tHowever, the paper has several deficiencies that prevent acceptance in its current form:\n141\t\n142\t1. **The 5-reference bibliography is a fatal formatting issue** that must be fixed before any venue submission.\n143\t2. **No head-to-head experimental comparison** with the closest related methods (A2C2, ReconVLA) leaves the core novelty claim empirically under-supported.\n144\t3. **No statistical reporting** (CIs, seed variance) undermines confidence in the headline numbers.\n145\t4. **Single VLA + single benchmark** limits the generality claims that pervade the text.\n146\t5. **The \"orthogonal gains\" claim** is tested on one training-time method only.\n147\t\n148\t**Path to acceptance**: Fix the bibliography. Add A2C2 and ReconVLA as experimental baselines. Report mean ± std across ≥3 seeds. Add K sensitivity analysis. Test on one additional VLA backbone. Reframe \"orthogonal\" to \"complementary to SRPO\" unless tested on multiple training-time methods. Clarify 45D arithmetic. Add key figures (ellipsoid visualization, ACI convergence, per-DOF correction patterns). With these additions, the paper's strong ablation design and practical value proposition could reach accept at CoRL.\n149\t\n150\t### 7. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n151\t\n152\tComparing against the full experiment design (review_context.yaml, 14 planned experiments):\n153\t\n154\t| Experiment | In Paper? | Assessment |\n155\t|-----------|-----------|------------|\n156\t| MVP Point-Reaching (plan_000) | ✅ Table 1 | Clean validation of core hypothesis |\n157\t| Full Pipeline (plan_001) | ✅ Table 2 | Main result, needs error bars |\n158\t| A3: No Conformal Features (plan_002) | ✅ Table 3 | Key ablation, convincing gap |\n159\t| A4: Scalar Uncertainty (plan_003) | ✅ Table 3 | Establishes clean gradient |\n160\t| SRPO Stacking (plan_004) | ✅ Table 2 | Headline SOTA result |\n161\t| COC Validation (plan_005) | ✅ Table 5 | Self-referential signal validated |\n162\t| A1: No ACI (plan_006) | ✅ Table 3 | ACI contribution isolated |\n163\t| A2: No CRM (plan_007) | ✅ Table 3 | Selection vs. correction quantified |\n164\t| Per-Perturbation (plan_008) | ✅ Table 4 | Thorough, with p-values |\n165\t| A5: Bonferroni (plan_009) | ✅ §4.7 | Joint > Bonferroni confirmed |\n166\t| A6: COC vs Episode (plan_010) | ✅ §4.6 | Convergence speed is primary advantage |\n167\t| A7: Epistemic vs Aleatoric (plan_011) | ✅ Table 6 | Strong conceptual contribution |\n168\t| Feature Importance (plan_012) | ✅ Table 7, §4.9 | Mechanistic evidence |\n169\t| SnapFlow (plan_013) | ✅ Table 8 | Practical speedup result |\n170\t\n171\t**Coverage: 14/14 experiments reported.** The experiment design is comprehensive and all planned experiments appear in the paper. The primary gaps are in reporting rigor (statistics, figures) and missing external baselines, not in experiment coverage.\n172\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CbMGLemWBkKWmY689v1Ncm","session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"6a317164-2962-44f3-a8de-a4b7edc1267c","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:22:07.018Z"}
