{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018u8WYsabYAotQJkcHbCaF7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n4\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage[expansion=false]{microtype}\n12\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\n15\t\\definecolor{failred}{RGB}{180,40,40}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\fail}[1]{\\textcolor{failred}{#1}}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{When Does Multivariate Conformal Prediction Help VLA Action Correction?\\\\\n19\t\\large A Diagnostic Study of the $K$-vs-Dimension Failure Mode}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{\n22\tAnonymous Authors\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t\\date{CoRL 2026 Workshop Submission}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t\\begin{abstract}\n31\tFlow-matching Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models naturally produce $K$ action samples, enabling conformal prediction (CP) to quantify per-degree-of-freedom (DOF) uncertainty via multivariate prediction regions.\n32\tA natural hypothesis is that these structured, per-DOF conformal statistics---encoding which action dimensions are uncertain and how they co-vary---can serve as features for a learned Conformal Residual Module (CRM) to perform dimension-selective action correction.\n33\tWe test this hypothesis systematically on LIBERO-Plus, a 7-DOF robotic manipulation benchmark with diverse distribution shifts.\n34\tOur central finding is \\textbf{negative}: at the $K{=}10$ sample budget typical of real-time VLA inference, Ledoit-Wolf covariance shrinkage intensity reaches 0.71, collapsing the $7{\\times}7$ sample covariance toward the identity matrix and destroying the cross-DOF structure that multivariate CP is designed to capture.\n35\tConsequently, per-DOF conformal features contribute only 0.8 percentage points (pp) beyond a generic observation-conditioned residual baseline (within noise), scalar uncertainty performs on par with 45-dimensional per-DOF features, and online Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) driven by Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) produces negligible volume narrowing ($6.2\\%$).\n36\tWe characterize the failure mode as a $K$-vs-dimension ratio problem: effective multivariate CP requires $K/d^2 \\gg 1$, while the VLA operating point of $K{=}10, d{=}7$ yields $K/d^2 \\approx 0.2$.\n37\tOur results delineate the regime boundary where per-DOF conformal features transition from informative (3-DOF, verified in a synthetic setting with shrinkage intensity 0.32) to uninformative (7-DOF), providing actionable guidance for future work on uncertainty-conditioned action correction.\n38\t\\end{abstract}\n39\t\n40\t\\section{Introduction}\n41\t\n42\tFlow-matching VLA models~\\citep{black2024pi0,pertsch2025fast,wen2025tinyvla} generate actions through an iterative denoising process that naturally produces $K$ stochastic samples per inference step.\n43\tThis multi-sample property has motivated several works to apply conformal prediction (CP)~\\citep{vovk2005algorithmic,angelopoulos2021gentle} for uncertainty quantification in robotic action spaces, either for sample selection~\\citep{luo2024reconvla}, policy gating~\\citep{dixit2024ups}, or planning-loop integration~\\citep{lindemann2023safe}.\n44\t\n45\tA compelling extension is \\emph{multivariate} CP: rather than reducing $K$ samples to a scalar uncertainty score, one can estimate per-DOF conformal radii, cross-DOF covariance structure, and ellipsoidal prediction regions that encode \\emph{which} action dimensions are uncertain and \\emph{how} they co-vary.\n46\tThe hypothesis motivating this work is that these structured per-DOF features can serve as input to a learned Conformal Residual Module (CRM), enabling \\emph{dimension-selective} action correction---aggressively correcting high-uncertainty DOFs while preserving confident predictions.\n47\t\n48\tWe term the full system \\textbf{ACR-VLA} (Adaptive Conformal Refinement for VLAs) and test it on LIBERO-Plus~\\citep{srpo2025}, a 7-DOF benchmark with seven categories of distribution shift.\n49\tOur investigation reveals a \\textbf{fundamental scaling barrier}: the $K{=}10$ sample budget available at real-time inference rates is insufficient for reliable multivariate covariance estimation in 7 dimensions.\n50\tLedoit-Wolf shrinkage~\\citep{ledoit2004well} compensates for the rank-deficient sample covariance by pulling it $71\\%$ toward the identity matrix, destroying the off-diagonal structure that is the theoretical motivation for per-DOF features.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n53\t\\begin{enumerate}\n54\t    \\item We identify and characterize the $K$-vs-dimension failure mode for multivariate CP in VLA action correction, establishing that the transition from informative to uninformative conformal features occurs around shrinkage intensity $\\sim$0.5 (corresponding to $K/d^2 \\approx 1$).\n55\t    \\item We provide a comprehensive ablation study (8 ablations, 11 experimental conditions) isolating the contribution of each ACR-VLA component, conclusively demonstrating that the 3.1pp LIBERO-Plus improvement is attributable to generic observation-conditioned residual learning, not conformal features.\n56\t    \\item We show that online Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC)-driven ACI adaptation is ineffective in the flow-matching setting due to denoising stochasticity dominating inter-chunk disagreement, and characterize when per-step self-referential signals break down.\n57\t\\end{enumerate}\n58\t\n59\t\\section{Background and Method}\n60\t\n61\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n62\t\n63\tWe consider a flow-matching VLA $\\pi_\\theta$ that, given observation $o_t$ and language instruction $l$, generates an action chunk $\\mathbf{a}_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times d}$ where $H$ is the chunk horizon and $d{=}7$ is the DOF count (6 joint positions + 1 gripper).\n64\tBy running the denoising process $K$ times with independent noise initializations, we obtain samples $\\{\\mathbf{a}_t^{(k)}\\}_{k=1}^K$.\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Conformal Geometry Statistics (CGS)}\n67\t\n68\tFrom $K$ samples, we compute per-DOF conformal features:\n69\t\\begin{itemize}\n70\t    \\item \\textbf{Per-DOF sample statistics}: mean $\\hat{\\mu}_j$, standard deviation $\\hat{\\sigma}_j$ for each DOF $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ ($2d{=}14$ features).\n71\t    \\item \\textbf{Ledoit-Wolf covariance}: $\\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\text{LW}} = \\alpha I + (1-\\alpha) \\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\text{sample}}$ where $\\alpha \\in [0,1]$ is the optimal shrinkage intensity.\n72\t    \\item \\textbf{Conformal radii}: per-DOF Mahalanobis radii from the $(1-\\delta)$-level prediction region ($d{=}7$ features).\n73\t    \\item \\textbf{Ellipsoid volume and shape}: $\\log\\det(\\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\text{LW}})$ and upper-triangular covariance entries ($1 + \\binom{d}{2}{=}22$ features).\n74\t    \\item \\textbf{Condition number}: $\\kappa(\\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\text{LW}})$ (1 feature).\n75\t\\end{itemize}\n76\tThe full CGS feature vector $\\mathbf{f}_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^{45}$ concatenates these statistics (we note 1 additional scalar for the shrinkage intensity $\\alpha$ itself).\n77\t\n78\t\\subsection{Conformal Residual Module (CRM)}\n79\t\n80\tThe CRM is a lightweight MLP ($\\sim$131K parameters) that predicts an action correction:\n81\t\\begin{equation}\n82\t    \\Delta\\mathbf{a}_t = \\text{CRM}(\\phi(o_t),\\; \\bar{\\mathbf{a}}_t,\\; \\mathbf{f}_t)\n83\t\\end{equation}\n84\twhere $\\phi(o_t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{128}$ is a frozen observation embedding, $\\bar{\\mathbf{a}}_t = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum_k \\mathbf{a}_t^{(k)}$ is the sample mean, and $\\mathbf{f}_t$ is the 45D CGS feature vector.\n85\tThe corrected action is $\\tilde{\\mathbf{a}}_t = \\bar{\\mathbf{a}}_t + \\Delta\\mathbf{a}_t$.\n86\t\n87\t\\subsection{Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) and Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI)}\n88\t\n89\tCOC exploits temporal overlap between consecutive action chunks: if actions $\\mathbf{a}_{t}$ and $\\mathbf{a}_{t+1}$ overlap in $H_{\\text{overlap}}$ timesteps, we measure consistency as the fraction of overlapping steps where both chunks' conformal regions contain the other's prediction.\n90\tACI~\\citep{gibbs2021adaptive} uses COC as a per-step coverage proxy to adaptively update the conformal threshold $\\hat{\\alpha}_t$ without ground-truth labels:\n91\t\\begin{equation}\n92\t    \\hat{\\alpha}_{t+1} = \\hat{\\alpha}_t + \\gamma(\\text{COC}_t - (1-\\delta))\n93\t\\end{equation}\n94\twhere $\\gamma$ is the step size and $\\delta$ is the target miscoverage rate.\n95\t\n96\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Base VLA.} SmolVLA, a flow-matching VLA with $K{=}10$ denoising samples and action chunk horizon $H{=}10$.\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} LIBERO (nominal, 10 tasks) and LIBERO-Plus (L3--L5, 7 perturbation categories: camera noise, Gaussian noise, robot-state offset, lighting, background, layout, language rephrase).\n101\t\n102\t\\paragraph{Training.} CRM is trained on LIBERO demonstrations augmented with synthetic perturbations (camera noise, Gaussian noise, robot-state offset) using expert demonstrations as correction targets.\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} We compare against the base VLA and report ablations systematically: A1 (no ACI), A2 (no CRM, sample selection only), A3 (CRM without conformal features), A4 (scalar uncertainty), A5 (per-DOF Bonferroni vs joint Mahalanobis), A6 (episode-level vs COC-driven ACI), A7 (DiU aleatoric vs CGS epistemic features).\n105\t\n106\t\\section{Results}\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Overall Performance}\n109\t\n110\tTable~\\ref{tab:main} summarizes the main results.\n111\tACR-VLA achieves a modest 3.1pp improvement on LIBERO-Plus over the base VLA, but incurs a 2.8pp degradation on nominal LIBERO.\n112\t\n113\t\\begin{table}[h]\n114\t\\centering\n115\t\\caption{ACR-VLA vs.\\ base VLA on LIBERO benchmarks. SR = success rate (\\%).}\n116\t\\label{tab:main}\n117\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n118\t\\toprule\n119\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{LIBERO (nominal)} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Plus (L3--L5)} \\\\\n120\t\\midrule\n121\tSmolVLA (base) & 96.5 & 62.0 \\\\\n122\tACR-VLA (full) & 93.7 & 65.1 \\\\\n123\t$\\Delta$ & \\fail{$-$2.8} & +3.1 \\\\\n124\t\\bottomrule\n125\t\\end{tabular}\n126\t\\end{table}\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{SRPO stacking.}\n129\tWhen stacked on SRPO~\\citep{srpo2025} (82.1\\% LIBERO-Plus), ACR-VLA yields only 1.3pp additional gain (83.4\\%), below the 2pp significance threshold, with 1.4pp nominal degradation ($97.2 \\to 95.8$\\%).\n130\tThe marginal return suggests ACR-VLA's residual correction overlaps with patterns already learned during SRPO's training-time optimization.\n131\t\n132\t\\subsection{The Core Negative Result: Conformal Features Are Uninformative at $K{=}10$, $d{=}7$}\n133\t\n134\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_core} presents the make-or-break ablations for the per-DOF conformal features hypothesis.\n135\t\n136\t\\begin{table}[h]\n137\t\\centering\n138\t\\caption{Ablation study isolating conformal feature contribution. Kill criterion: full $-$ A3 $<$ 2pp.}\n139\t\\label{tab:ablation_core}\n140\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n141\t\\toprule\n142\t& \\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Plus SR} & \\textbf{Nominal SR} \\\\\n143\t\\midrule\n144\t& SmolVLA (base) & 62.0 & 96.5 \\\\\n145\tA3 & CRM, no conformal features & 64.3 & 94.5 \\\\\n146\tA4 & CRM, scalar uncertainty & 65.3 & 94.1 \\\\\n147\t& ACR-VLA (full, 45D conformal) & 65.1 & 93.7 \\\\\n148\t\\midrule\n149\t& Gap: full $-$ A3 & \\fail{+0.8} & $-$0.8 \\\\\n150\t& Gap: full $-$ A4 (per-DOF vs scalar) & \\fail{$-$0.2} & $-$0.4 \\\\\n151\t\\bottomrule\n152\t\\end{tabular}\n153\t\\end{table}\n154\t\n155\tThe results are unambiguous.\n156\tThe observation-conditioned residual (A3) accounts for 2.3pp of the 3.1pp total gain.\n157\tAdding 45D per-DOF conformal features (full ACR-VLA) contributes only 0.8pp beyond A3---below noise given LIBERO-Plus inter-seed variance of $\\sim$1.5pp.\n158\tCritically, scalar uncertainty (A4, 1 dimension) performs \\emph{on par or marginally better} than per-DOF features (45 dimensions), directly contradicting the hypothesis that multivariate structure provides additional signal.\n159\t\n160\t\\paragraph{Gradient-based feature importance.}\n161\tCRM gradient analysis confirms the mechanism: observation embedding features receive $5.25\\times$ higher average gradient magnitude (0.042) than conformal features (0.008).\n162\tNo conformal feature appears among the top 20 features by gradient magnitude.\n163\tConformal feature gradients show near-zero variance across perturbation types (std = 0.002), indicating CRM has learned to ignore them uniformly.\n164\t\n165\t\\subsection{Diagnosing the Failure: Covariance Collapse}\n166\t\n167\t\\paragraph{Shrinkage as the smoking gun.}\n168\tLedoit-Wolf optimal shrinkage intensity is 0.71 at $K{=}10$, $d{=}7$, meaning the estimated covariance is $\\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\text{LW}} = 0.71 I + 0.29 \\hat{\\Sigma}_{\\text{sample}}$.\n169\tThe off-diagonal elements average 0.06 in magnitude, and the condition number is 14.2 (vs.\\ $\\sim$5 in the 3-DOF synthetic setting where shrinkage was 0.32).\n170\tThe max-to-min per-DOF conformal radius ratio is only 1.08---the ellipsoid is nearly a sphere.\n171\t\n172\t\\paragraph{CGS vs Bonferroni (A5).}\n173\tJoint Mahalanobis ellipsoids (CGS) provide no advantage over independent per-DOF intervals with Bonferroni correction: 65.1\\% vs 65.0\\%, gap = 0.1pp.\n174\tThe volume ratio is 1.03---the two region geometries are functionally identical because the near-identity covariance produces near-spherical Mahalanobis ellipsoids.\n175\t\n176\t\\paragraph{Scaling regime.}\n177\tThe effective sample-to-dimension ratio governs covariance estimation quality.\n178\tFor covariance estimation of a $d$-dimensional distribution from $K$ samples, the degrees of freedom for $\\binom{d}{2}$ off-diagonal entries scale as $K - d$.\n179\tAt $K{=}10$, $d{=}7$: only 3 effective degrees of freedom for 21 off-diagonal entries.\n180\tThe resulting $K/d^2 \\approx 0.2$ is well below the $K/d^2 \\geq 1$ threshold we observe empirically for informative multivariate CP.\n181\t\n182\t\\subsection{COC-Driven ACI Is Ineffective}\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table}[h]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\caption{COC signal quality and ACI adaptation effectiveness.}\n187\t\\label{tab:coc}\n188\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n189\t\\toprule\n190\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{Status} \\\\\n191\t\\midrule\n192\tSpearman $\\rho$ (COC vs.\\ task success) & 0.19 & $\\geq 0.3$ & \\fail{FAIL} \\\\\n193\tEllipsoid volume narrowing (ACI vs.\\ static) & 6.2\\% & $\\geq 10\\%$ & \\fail{FAIL} \\\\\n194\tACI convergence (episodes) & 45 & --- & --- \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t\\end{table}\n198\t\n199\tCOC provides a weak but statistically significant correlation with task success ($\\rho = 0.19$, $p = 0.04$), insufficient for reliable per-step coverage estimation.\n200\tThe fundamental issue: flow-matching VLAs generate each chunk via stochastic denoising, and inter-chunk disagreement is dominated by sampling noise rather than epistemic uncertainty."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U6BBV3mx4ktHfYbR5GdLQQ","session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"285f6796-439d-44a9-b300-bd100afadc1d","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:22:14.358Z"}
