{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0152tndrFFSdkTxB1v89X3mG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260513T023849\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: 5/10 — Weak Reject (Marginally Below Acceptance)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-13T03:19:16.171806+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# ACR-VLA Dream Review Report\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** ACR-VLA: Inference-Time Action Correction via Structured Conformal Uncertainty Features for Vision-Language-Action Models\n11\t**Venue:** CoRL 2026 | **Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n12\t**Date:** 2026-05-13\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Reviewer Panel\n17\t\n18\t| Reviewer | Focus | Overall Score |\n19\t|----------|-------|---------------|\n20\t| R1 | Methods + Experiments | 4/10 |\n21\t| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 5/10 |\n22\t| **Consensus** | | **5/10 — Marginally Below Acceptance** |\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## 1. Paper Summary\n27\t\n28\tACR-VLA proposes an inference-time action correction framework for flow-matching VLAs with three components: (1) a Conformal Gaussian Summarizer (CGS) extracting 45D per-DOF conformal features from K=10 flow-matching samples via Ledoit-Wolf covariance + multivariate CP; (2) a 131K-parameter Conformal Residual Module (CRM) conditioned on these features for dimension-selective action correction; (3) Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) with Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC) for online threshold recalibration. On LIBERO-Plus, ACR-VLA improves SmolVLA from 62.3% to 70.5% (+8.2%), with statistically significant gains in 4/7 perturbation dimensions. When stacked on SRPO (82.1%), it reaches 85.0% (+2.9%). Ablations decompose: CRM alone +3.5%, scalar uncertainty +2.6%, per-DOF +2.1%.\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Strengths (Consensus)\n33\t\n34\t**S1. Well-structured ablation decomposition (R1, R2).** The A3/A4/Full hierarchy (62.3→65.8→68.4→70.5) cleanly decomposes contributions into three additive components. The finding that combined uncertainty contribution (+4.7%) exceeds residual-only (+3.5%) supports the central claim. This is the paper's strongest evidence and both reviewers found it convincing.\n35\t\n36\t**S2. Epistemic vs. aleatoric distinction is a genuine insight (R1, R2).** The A7 ablation showing 7.42× OOD/ID variance ratio for CGS epistemic features vs. 1.31× for DiU aleatoric features provides clean mechanistic evidence. This is a useful contribution to the broader uncertainty-in-robotics community, independent of the specific method.\n37\t\n38\t**S3. Honest and well-calibrated claims (R1, R2).** Both reviewers found the transparent per-perturbation reporting (showing 3/7 non-significant dimensions), the acknowledgment that per-DOF is \"additionally beneficial rather than essential,\" and the characterization of COC as \"promising rather than reliable\" to be unusually honest. This credibility is rare and appreciated.\n39\t\n40\t**S4. Principled statistical methodology (R1).** Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage is the correct choice for p=7, n=10 (p/n=0.7). Mahalanobis-based joint nonconformity scores avoid Bonferroni conservatism, validated by A5 ablation (28% smaller regions at matched coverage).\n41\t\n42\t**S5. COC as self-referential signal is creative (R1, R2).** Exploiting temporal overlap structure of action chunks for coverage feedback without external supervision is a non-obvious idea with genuine deployment relevance.\n43\t\n44\t**S6. Practical engineering awareness (R1, R2).** 131K params, 3.2ms CRM overhead, SnapFlow compatibility (3.1× speedup at −0.7%), and frozen-base-VLA deployment model show deployment awareness unusual for an academic paper.\n45\t\n46\t---\n47\t\n48\t## 3. Weaknesses (Consensus)\n49\t\n50\t### Major Issues\n51\t\n52\t**W1. No head-to-head comparison with any external baseline (R1: Major, R2: Critical for novelty).** The experimental tables contain only SmolVLA, ACR-VLA variants, and SRPO. A2C2 (closest residual correction), ReconVLA (closest CP-based selection), UPS (CP gating), and PLD (residual RL) are discussed in Related Work but absent from experiments. The A3 ablation approximates a generic residual policy but is not equivalent to the actual methods. Without head-to-head comparisons, the reader cannot determine whether ACR-VLA's gains stem from conformal features specifically or from any inference-time correction applied to SmolVLA. **Both reviewers identified this as the single most critical experimental gap.**\n53\t\n54\t**W2. Per-DOF advantage is modest relative to paper framing (R1, R2).** The headline contribution is dimension-selective correction via per-DOF features, yet the actual per-DOF advantage over scalar is only +2.1% (A4→Full). The paper's title, method name, and core claims are built around per-DOF structure, which contributes the smallest increment in the A3→A4→Full decomposition (+3.5/+2.6/+2.1). R2 notes this \"inverts the rhetorical structure: the paper emphasizes the least impactful component.\" The paper's own honest framing partially mitigates this, but the mismatch between emphasis and evidence remains.\n55\t\n56\t**W3. No confidence intervals on main results (R1: Major).** Tables 1, 3, and 4 report point estimates only despite 5-seed evaluation. Error bars appear only in the per-perturbation table (Table 2). The +2.1% per-DOF advantage, +2.9% SRPO stacking gain, and +2.7% ACI contribution are all small enough to be potentially within noise. Statistical significance of these margins is unverifiable.\n57\t\n58\t**W4. No K sensitivity analysis (R1: Major, R2 concurs).** K=10 is fixed throughout with zero ablation. Given the ~15-20% CGS feature quality degradation from oracle to K=10 covariance, understanding performance at K=5 and K=20 is critical for both scientific understanding and deployment guidance. This is the most important practical hyperparameter.\n59\t\n60\t**W5. Augmentation memorization confound (R1: Major).** The near-perfect correlation (r=0.91, noted in design context) between synthetic augmentation coverage and ACR-VLA gain raises a fundamental question: is CRM learning to use conformal features for genuine distributional reasoning, or memorizing correction patterns for specific training augmentations? A held-out perturbation experiment (train without camera augmentation, evaluate on camera shift) would disentangle these. Without this, the \"dimension-selective correction via conformal features\" narrative may be partially confounded.\n61\t\n62\t### Moderate Issues\n63\t\n64\t**W6. Single VLA backbone limits generality (R1, R2).** All experiments use SmolVLA only. Claims about applicability to \"any flow-matching VLA\" are unsupported. CGS feature quality likely varies with model scale and architecture.\n65\t\n66\t**W7. Novelty is primarily combination (R2).** Each component (Ledoit-Wolf, Mahalanobis CP, ACI, residual correction) is well-established. The paper's contribution is combining them. R2 notes improvements are additive rather than synergistic — no evidence of super-additive interaction between components.\n67\t\n68\t**W8. Narrow novelty claim (R2).** \"First to use multivariate CP statistics as direct input features for a learned correction module\" is technically true but defines such a precise category that being first is nearly tautological. Using per-DOF sample statistics from flow-matching as MLP conditioning is not fundamentally different from ensemble disagreement or MC-dropout variance as residual policy inputs.\n69\t\n70\t**W9. SRPO stacking gain near kill threshold (R1, R2).** +2.9% barely exceeds the pre-specified 2% kill criterion. Without error bars, statistical significance is questionable. The 2.5% nominal drop on SRPO (larger than 2.2% on base VLA) suggests CRM degrades already-precise SRPO actions more.\n71\t\n72\t**W10. COC practical value is limited (R1, R2).** ρ=0.38 explains only ~14% of task outcome variance. ACI convergence requires ~148 episodes (vs. 42 for episode-level). Episode-level ACI outperforms COC-based by 1.3%. COC is effectively a weaker alternative to episode-level ACI, not a superior signal.\n73\t\n74\t**W11. Missing related work (R2).** \"Shifting Uncertainty to Critical Moments\" (per-DOF instability indicators for VLAs) is the most directly related concurrent work on per-DOF uncertainty features for robot actions and is not discussed. Several other inference-time correction methods (PDF, VLA-in-the-Loop) are also missing. Bibliography has only 14 entries, several placeholder-quality.\n75\t\n76\t### Minor Issues\n77\t\n78\t**W12. 45D feature vector arithmetic inconsistency (R1).** Section 3.2 lists cross-DOF covariance as 21D (upper triangle of 7×7), but Table 7 lists 30D for the same category. The two decompositions do not reconcile.\n79\t\n80\t**W13. \"Conformal\" label is misleading (R1, R2).** Once CGS features pass through CRM, no formal coverage guarantee transfers to the corrected action. \"Conformal\" in the method name may create expectations the method does not deliver.\n81\t\n82\t**W14. CRM architecture details insufficient (R1).** Hidden dimensions, activations, normalization, learning rate, batch size, epochs, train/val split, and training curves are not reported.\n83\t\n84\t---\n85\t\n86\t## 4. Key Questions for Authors\n87\t\n88\t1. **External baselines**: Can you run A2C2 and ReconVLA on SmolVLA + LIBERO-Plus? If A2C2 exceeds 65.8% (A3 result), the conformal feature contribution narrows.\n89\t\n90\t2. **Held-out perturbation**: If CRM is trained without camera augmentation, what is the gain on camera shift? This isolates conformal features from augmentation memorization.\n91\t\n92\t3. **Error bars**: What are the 95% CIs on the +2.1% per-DOF gap, +2.9% SRPO stacking, and +2.7% ACI contribution?\n93\t\n94\t4. **K sensitivity**: How does performance vary at K ∈ {5, 10, 15, 20}? What is the cost-performance Pareto frontier?\n95\t\n96\t5. **45D reconciliation**: Is cross-DOF covariance 21D or 30D? Please reconcile the two decompositions.\n97\t\n98\t6. **Second VLA**: Have you tested on pi0 or another flow-matching VLA?\n99\t\n100\t7. **Feature reduction**: Per-DOF std (42%) and conformal radius (31%) are highly correlated (radius = std × √q). Does a reduced feature set achieve comparable results?\n101\t\n102\t8. **Relationship to \"Shifting Uncertainty to Critical Moments\"**: How does this concurrent work on per-DOF instability indicators relate to your CGS features?\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n107\t\n108\tAll 13 imagined experiments from the design document are covered in the paper:\n109\t\n110\t| Experiment | Covered | Notes |\n111\t|-----------|---------|-------|\n112\t| Full pipeline (plan_001) | ✓ | Table 1 |\n113\t| A3 ablation (plan_002) | ✓ | Table 3 |\n114\t| A4 ablation (plan_003) | ✓ | Table 3 |\n115\t| SRPO stacking (plan_004) | ✓ | Table 1 |\n116\t| COC validation (plan_005) | ✓ | Table 6 |\n117\t| A1 ablation (plan_006) | ✓ | Table 4 |\n118\t| A2 ablation (plan_007) | ✓ | Table 4 |\n119\t| Per-perturbation (plan_008) | ✓ | Table 2 |\n120\t| A5 Bonferroni (plan_009) | ✓ | Table 4 |\n121\t| A6 episode vs COC (plan_010) | ✓ | Table 4 |\n122\t| A7 epistemic vs aleatoric (plan_011) | ✓ | Table 5 |\n123\t| Feature importance (plan_012) | ✓ | Table 7 |\n124\t| SnapFlow (plan_013) | ✓ | Table 8 |\n125\t\n126\tCoverage is complete, but the paper is dense with internal ablations while lacking any external baseline comparison.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## 6. Scores\n131\t\n132\t### R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n133\t| Dimension | Score |\n134\t|-----------|-------|\n135\t| Soundness | 2.5/4 |\n136\t| Empirical Methodology | 2/4 |\n137\t| Clarity | 2.5/4 |\n138\t| Overall | 4/10 |\n139\t| Confidence | 4/5 |\n140\t\n141\t### R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n142\t| Dimension | Score |\n143\t|-----------|-------|\n144\t| Novelty | 2/4 |\n145\t| Related Work Coverage | 2/4 |\n146\t| Significance | 2/4 |\n147\t| Overall | 5/10 |\n148\t| Confidence | 4/5 |\n149\t\n150\t### Consensus Assessment\n151\t\n152\t| Dimension | Score |\n153\t|-----------|-------|\n154\t| **Overall** | **5/10 — Marginally Below Acceptance Threshold** |\n155\t| Soundness | 2.5/4 |\n156\t| Novelty | 2/4 |\n157\t| Empirical Rigor | 2/4 |\n158\t| Clarity | 2.5/4 |\n159\t| Significance | 2/4 |\n160\t| Confidence | 4/5 |\n161\t\n162\t**Decision: Weak Reject**\n163\t\n164\t---\n165\t\n166\t## 7. Verdict & Actionable Recommendations\n167\t\n168\tThe paper identifies a genuine gap (structured uncertainty features for inference-time VLA correction) and proposes a well-engineered solution with commendable honesty about limitations. The ablation hierarchy is the paper's strongest contribution. However, the absence of any external baseline comparison is disqualifying at the current stage — without knowing how ACR-VLA compares to A2C2, ReconVLA, or UPS, the contribution cannot be properly assessed. The per-DOF advantage (+2.1%), while real, is modest relative to the paper's emphasis and potentially within noise without error bars.\n169\t\n170\t**To reach acceptance, the paper needs:**\n171\t\n172\t1. **[Critical] Head-to-head with A2C2 and ReconVLA** on the same SmolVLA + LIBERO-Plus setup\n173\t2. **[Critical] Error bars (mean ± std) on all main tables** with significance tests for key margins\n174\t3. **[Important] K sensitivity sweep** (K ∈ {5, 10, 15, 20})\n175\t4. **[Important] Held-out perturbation test** to address augmentation memorization confound\n176\t5. **[Important] Second VLA backbone** (e.g., pi0) for generality\n177\t6. **[Moderate] Fix 45D feature arithmetic inconsistency** and add CRM architecture details\n178\t7. **[Moderate] Expand bibliography and discuss concurrent per-DOF uncertainty work**\n179\t8. **[Moderate] Reframe narrative** to match evidence: the residual + uncertainty conditioning story (+4.7%) is stronger than the per-DOF story (+2.1%)\n180\t\n181\tThe paper has clear potential for CoRL poster with these additions. The ablation design, epistemic vs. aleatoric analysis, and honest reporting are strengths that a revised version should preserve.\n182\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CbMGLemWBkKWmY689v1Ncm","session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"48cc76bf-b9ae-4b16-8cb4-c0b2e4f7aa38","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:22:16.067Z"}
