{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CbMGLemWBkKWmY689v1Ncm","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the project. The user's prompt describes a **negative-result version** of this paper -- where all three main claims fail their pre-registered kill criteria. This is a different framing from the dream reviews which simulated positive results. Let me now produce the R2 review for this negative-result paper.\n\n---\n\n### R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Summary**\n\nThis paper presents ACR-VLA, a three-component pipeline (CGS, ACI+COC, CRM) applying multivariate conformal prediction to VLA action correction, framed as a systematic negative-result empirical study. All three pre-registered claims fail their kill criteria: per-DOF conformal features provide negligible benefit over scalar/none (0.8pp, p=0.38), COC yields only weak correlation with success (rho=0.18), and SRPO stacking is not significant (+1.1pp, p=0.29). The root cause is identified as covariance degeneracy (Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage 0.91, effective rank 1.3) stemming from K=10 samples in 7D. The paper contributes precise failure-mode diagnostics rather than a working method.\n\n**Strengths**\n\n1. **Pre-registered kill criteria are laudable.** Defining quantitative thresholds (2pp for A3, rho 0.3 for COC, 2pp for SRPO stacking) before running experiments and honestly reporting failure against them is a practice the community should encourage. This raises the paper's scientific credibility relative to typical negative results reported post-hoc.\n\n2. **Root cause analysis is precise and educational.** The covariance degeneracy diagnosis -- K=10 samples in p=7 dimensions yielding p/n=0.7, forcing Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage to alpha=0.91 with effective rank 1.3 -- is a clean, quantitative explanation. This is the paper's strongest technical contribution: it concretely characterizes when multivariate CP becomes uninformative, providing a transferable lesson.\n\n3. **The per-perturbation analysis revealing augmentation-bounded correction is informative.** Showing that gains (where they exist) concentrate on perturbation types covered by synthetic augmentation, while uncovered types (layout, language) show no benefit, is a useful empirical finding about the limitations of inference-time correction trained on synthetic augmentations.\n\n4. **The experimental scope is reasonable for a negative-result study.** Eleven experiments on two benchmarks (LIBERO, LIBERO-Plus) with clearly defined ablations provide sufficient evidence to support the negative conclusions.\n\n5. **The paper addresses a genuine gap in the literature.** At the time of writing, no prior work had attempted to use structured per-DOF conformal statistics as learned correction features for VLA actions. Documenting that this does not work in the K=10 regime is useful.\n\n**Weaknesses**\n\n1. **The core negative result is largely predictable from first principles (Major).** With K=10 samples and p=7 dimensions, p/n=0.7 is firmly in the regime where sample covariance matrices are known to be unreliable. The Marchenko-Pastur law predicts ~30-40% relative error in eigenvalue estimation. Any statistician would predict that Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage would dominate, collapsing the covariance toward the identity and destroying the per-DOF structure that the entire method relies upon. The \"surprise\" value of this negative result is low. The paper acknowledges this in the paper framing (reviewer concern #1) and even identifies Ledoit-Wolf as a known risk in the MVP, yet proceeds with K=10 anyway without first establishing whether K=10 is sufficient. A more informative study would have swept K in {5, 10, 20, 50, 100} to find the threshold where multivariate CP becomes informative, turning a predictable negative result into actionable guidance.\n\n2. **No exploration of the positive regime diminishes contribution (Major).** The paper identifies covariance degeneracy as the root cause but does not test K>10. If K=20 or K=30 produces effective rank >3 and significant per-DOF benefits, the contribution shifts from \"multivariate CP does not help\" to \"multivariate CP requires K>N_threshold, and here is the cost-benefit curve.\" Without this, the paper answers a narrow question (does K=10 work?) rather than the broader one (when does multivariate CP help?). The title asks \"When Does Multivariate CP Help?\" but the paper only shows when it does not.\n\n3. **Only 10 references is insufficient for a venue like CoRL (Major).** Even for a negative-result paper, the related work section must comprehensively cover the space. Key omissions include: (a) \"Shifting Uncertainty to Critical Moments\" (arXiv:2603.18342) -- the most directly related work on per-DOF uncertainty for VLA actions; (b) TT-VLA (arXiv:2601.06748) -- test-time adaptation for VLAs; (c) PDF (arXiv:2604.18107) -- verifier-free test-time correction; (d) CRT (arXiv:2602.01158) -- observation-level corruption restoration; (e) SnapFlow (arXiv:2604.05656) -- relevant because 1-step flow matching could dramatically change K sample diversity; (f) Conformalized Teleoperation (RSS 2024) -- closest prior application of ACI to robot actions; (g) VLA-GSE, Cosmos-Policy, and other LIBERO-Plus baselines establishing the current SOTA landscape; (h) broader ensemble/MC-dropout uncertainty literature in robotics; (i) CP surveys establishing methodological context. Ten references signals either incomplete scholarship or a rushed submission.\n\n4. **No head-to-head comparison with external baselines (Major).** The paper discusses ReconVLA, A2C2, UPS, and SAFE in related work but does not experimentally compare against any of them. Even for a negative-result paper, showing that ACR-VLA underperforms or merely matches simpler baselines (e.g., ReconVLA's scalar CP selection) would strengthen the argument. Without these comparisons, the reader cannot determine whether the failure is specific to the multivariate CP approach or whether all inference-time CP-based corrections struggle similarly.\n\n5. **The three stated contributions are thin individually (Moderate).** Contribution 1 (negative result + covariance diagnostics) is the only substantial one. Contribution 2 (COC failure mode, rho=0.18) is a single correlation statistic that could be reported in a paragraph. Contribution 3 (augmentation-bounded correction) is an observation, not a contribution -- it follows trivially from the fact that learned correction generalizes poorly beyond training distribution. Packaging these as three contributions inflates the perceived scope.\n\n6. **COC signal failure mode is underexplored (Moderate).** The paper reports rho=0.18 but does not adequately diagnose why COC fails. Is it because the covariance degeneracy produces uninformative conformal regions (making the overlap check meaningless)? Is it because consecutive VLA predictions are too correlated? Is it because action chunk overlap structure is fundamentally uninformative about execution quality? Without disentangling these, the COC failure is reported but not explained.\n\n7. **Claim 3 testing is weak (Moderate).** The SRPO stacking experiment (+1.1pp, p=0.29) tests only one training-time method. The paper cannot claim \"inference-time conformal correction provides no orthogonal value\" from a single stacking experiment, especially when the base method (ACR-VLA alone) already shows only marginal improvement. The stacking failure may simply inherit the base method's failure rather than revealing anything about orthogonality.\n\n8. **The MVP positive result creates a confusing narrative (Minor).** The paper framing and memory.md show that the 3D point-reaching MVP passed convincingly (per-DOF 20-38% better than scalar). The full paper then shows the VLA-scale experiments fail. The paper needs to clearly explain why the MVP succeeded but the full pipeline failed -- presumably the MVP used oracle or K=10 with p=3 (p/n=0.3, well-conditioned), while the full pipeline uses K=10 with p=7 (p/n=0.7, degenerate). This transition should be the paper's central analytical thread.\n\n**Questions for Authors**\n\n1. Why was K not swept beyond 10? The covariance degeneracy diagnosis points directly to K as the bottleneck. Even a post-hoc experiment with K=20 or K=50 on a representative subset would dramatically strengthen the paper's conclusions.\n\n2. What is the effective rank of the sample covariance at K=20? If effective rank rises above 3-4, does the per-DOF advantage re-emerge? This is the single most important missing experiment.\n\n3. How does the MVP (p=3, K=10 → p/n=0.3) relate to the negative VLA results (p=7, K=10 → p/n=0.7)? Is there a critical p/n threshold between 0.3 and 0.7 where the method transitions from working to failing?\n\n4. Have you considered alternative covariance estimators beyond Ledoit-Wolf (e.g., graphical lasso, factor models, or assuming diagonal + low-rank structure) that might preserve per-DOF information even at K=10?\n\n5. For the COC signal (rho=0.18): is this measured on degenerate conformal regions? If the regions are essentially spherical (due to shrinkage collapsing to identity), the overlap check is uninformative by construction -- this should be stated explicitly.\n\n6. The full pipeline shows +3pp on LIBERO-Plus but -4.2pp on nominal LIBERO. Does this mean the CRM learns to \"over-correct\" already-good predictions? What is the per-perturbation breakdown for the nominal regression?\n\n7. What is the inference latency for K>10? Is K=50 prohibitively expensive, or could batched generation on modern GPUs make it feasible?\n\n**Missing Related Work or Baselines**\n\nThe following are absent from the 10-reference bibliography and represent significant gaps:\n\n- \"Shifting Uncertainty to Critical Moments\" (arXiv:2603.18342) -- per-DOF instability indicators for VLAs, the most directly comparable concurrent work\n- TT-VLA (arXiv:2601.06748) -- test-time VLA adaptation, relevant for the inference-time correction comparison\n- A2C2 (arXiv:2509.23224) -- the closest residual correction baseline; cited in text but not in bibliography and not experimentally compared\n- UPS (arXiv:2602.22474) -- CP for robot policy correction; cited in text but not experimentally compared\n- Conformalized Teleoperation (RSS 2024, arXiv:2406.07767) -- ACI applied to robot actions\n- Learnable Conformal Prediction (arXiv:2509.21955) -- context-aware CP scores achieving tighter intervals\n- Cosmos-Policy / WAM robustness study (arXiv:2603.22078) -- establishes LIBERO-Plus SOTA context\n- PDF (arXiv:2604.18107) -- test-time perturbation learning for VLAs\n- SnapFlow (arXiv:2604.05656) -- 1-step flow matching relevant to sample diversity\n- CRT (arXiv:2602.01158) -- orthogonal corruption restoration approach\n- DiU (arXiv:2605.00941) -- single-pass uncertainty for flow matching; the epistemic vs. aleatoric distinction is relevant even in a negative paper\n- Ensemble uncertainty and MC-dropout literature in robotics\n- CP survey/tutorial references (Angelopoulos & Bates 2023, etc.)\n\nMissing experimental baselines: ReconVLA (scalar CP selection on the same setup), A2C2 (residual correction without CP features), vanilla K-sample mean (simplest multi-sample aggregation).\n\n**Minor Issues**\n\n- The paper title asks \"When Does Multivariate Conformal Prediction Help?\" but only characterizes when it does not help (K=10, p=7). The title overpromises relative to the content.\n- 45D feature dimensionality: the paper framing describes 45D (7 std + 7 radii + ... ) but later project documents indicate 40D (action_dim=6, H_eff=5). This inconsistency should be resolved.\n- The -4.2pp nominal regression is concerning and underexplored. A method that hurts nominal performance while providing marginal OOD improvement has negative practical value in many deployment scenarios.\n- The framing positions this as \"systematic\" but 11 experiments on one VLA backbone (SmolVLA) and one benchmark family (LIBERO) is not systematic in the usual sense of the word.\n\n**Scores:**\n- Novelty: 1.5/4 -- The negative result is largely predictable from the p/n=0.7 regime. The covariance diagnostics are precise but the finding itself (small K + moderate p = degenerate covariance) is well-known in statistics. No exploration of the positive regime (K>10) limits the novelty of the failure characterization.\n- Significance: 1.5/4 -- Practitioners will take away \"do not use K=10 multivariate CP in 7D,\" which is useful but narrow. The absence of a K sweep means the paper cannot provide actionable guidance on what K is sufficient. The community would benefit more from a \"when it works vs. when it doesn't\" study than a pure \"it doesn't work at K=10\" report.\n- Clarity: 2.5/4 -- The pre-registered kill criteria and experimental structure are clear. The narrative transition from positive MVP to negative full-scale results needs better handling. Writing quality appears adequate based on available materials.\n- Related Work Coverage: 1/4 -- Ten references is far below the minimum for a CoRL submission. Multiple directly relevant works are omitted from both the bibliography and experimental comparisons. The positioning against the landscape is textually reasonable but formally incomplete.\n- Overall (novelty/related work perspective): 3/10 -- The paper documents a predictable negative result without exploring the boundary conditions that would make it genuinely informative. The thin bibliography and absent baselines compound the limited novelty. A negative-result paper at CoRL needs to either (a) refute a widely-held belief with surprising evidence, or (b) provide comprehensive characterization of failure modes with actionable guidance for practitioners. This paper does neither convincingly: the covariance degeneracy at K=10 is foreseeable, and the lack of a K sweep means practitioners learn only that K=10 is insufficient, not what K is sufficient.\n- Confidence: 4/5 -- I have strong familiarity with conformal prediction, multivariate statistics, and the VLA robustness literature. The covariance degeneracy analysis is straightforward to evaluate. Minor uncertainty about implementation details that could affect the results."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae3053c7aca50ecd6 (use SendMessage with to: 'ae3053c7aca50ecd6' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 58155\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 138876</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"8df84902-565e-4683-9c2d-50f5fb05cf0a","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:23:53.934Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty, significance, and related work**. Produce a detailed, independent review.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=strong accept, 1=strong reject). Acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n- Criteria: originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance to robot learning, limitations.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Multivariate Conformal Prediction Help Robot Action Correction? A Systematic Empirical Study\"\n\nThis paper presents ACR-VLA, a pipeline applying multivariate conformal prediction (CP) to VLA action correction. The system has three components: (i) Conformal Geometry Score (CGS) extracting 45D per-DOF features from K=10 flow-matching samples, (ii) Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI) with self-referential Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC), and (iii) Conformal Residual Module (CRM) for learned action correction. The paper is framed as a systematic empirical study reporting negative results: all three main claims fail pre-registered kill criteria across 11 experiments on LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus.\n\n## Three Claims (all fail):\n1. Per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction superior to scalar/none.\n2. COC provides reliable self-referential calibration signal.\n3. Inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal value when stacked on training-time methods.\n\n## Key Negative Results:\n- A3 ablation: removing conformal features → 0.8pp drop, p=0.38 (kill criterion <2pp triggered)\n- A4: Per-DOF vs scalar: 0.3pp difference (noise)\n- COC-success correlation: ρ=0.18 (weak)\n- SRPO stacking: +1.1pp, p=0.29 (not significant)\n- Full pipeline: only +3pp on LIBERO-Plus but -4.2pp on nominal\n- Root cause: K=10 in 7D → Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage 0.91, effective rank 1.3 (covariance degeneracy)\n\n## Stated Contributions:\n1. Systematic negative result with precise covariance diagnostics\n2. COC signal failure mode characterization\n3. Per-perturbation analysis showing augmentation-bounded correction\n\n## Related Work Mentioned:\n- CP for robotics: ReconVLA (scalar nonconformity score), CP-in-the-Loop, UPS\n- Action correction: A2C2, PLD (residual policies without calibrated uncertainty), SAFE (OOD detection)\n- Robustness: RobustVLA, DiG-Flow, SRPO (training-time methods)\n- Multivariate CP: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage theory\n- References: 10 total (Vovk et al. 2005, Ledoit &amp; Wolf 2004, LIBERO-Plus Kim et al. 2024, ReconVLA Luo et al. 2024, SRPO Ren et al. 2024, Gibbs &amp; Candès 2021, π0 Black et al. 2024, A2C2 Liang et al. 2024, SAFE Dixit et al. 2024, DiG-Flow Wu et al. 2024)\n\n## Your Review Task\nAssess:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the negative result itself novel and surprising? Or is the covariance degeneracy outcome predictable from basic statistics (K=10, p=7 → p/n=0.7)?\n2. **Significance for CoRL**: Do these negative results provide sufficient value for the robot learning community? Is a negative-result empirical study appropriate for CoRL?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are key baselines and prior works missing? Is the positioning against A2C2/ReconVLA/SRPO fair?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-framed as an empirical study? Does it avoid burying the lede?\n5. **Writing quality and clarity**.\n6. **Reference quality**: Only 10 references — is this sufficient? Are there important missing citations?\n7. **Contribution granularity**: Are the three \"contributions\" substantial enough individually? Could they have been predicted a priori without running experiments?\n8. **Impact**: Will practitioners change behavior based on these findings?\n\nProduce your review in this format:\n\n### R2 Review: Novelty &amp; Related Work\n\n**Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths** (numbered list)\n\n**Weaknesses** (numbered list)\n\n**Questions for Authors** (numbered list)\n\n**Missing Related Work or Baselines**\n\n**Minor Issues**\n\n**Scores:**\n- Novelty: X/4\n- Significance: X/4\n- Clarity: X/4\n- Related Work Coverage: X/4\n- Overall (novelty/related work perspective): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n","agentId":"ae3053c7aca50ecd6","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the project. The user's prompt describes a **negative-result version** of this paper -- where all three main claims fail their pre-registered kill criteria. This is a different framing from the dream reviews which simulated positive results. Let me now produce the R2 review for this negative-result paper.\n\n---\n\n### R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Summary**\n\nThis paper presents ACR-VLA, a three-component pipeline (CGS, ACI+COC, CRM) applying multivariate conformal prediction to VLA action correction, framed as a systematic negative-result empirical study. All three pre-registered claims fail their kill criteria: per-DOF conformal features provide negligible benefit over scalar/none (0.8pp, p=0.38), COC yields only weak correlation with success (rho=0.18), and SRPO stacking is not significant (+1.1pp, p=0.29). The root cause is identified as covariance degeneracy (Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage 0.91, effective rank 1.3) stemming from K=10 samples in 7D. The paper contributes precise failure-mode diagnostics rather than a working method.\n\n**Strengths**\n\n1. **Pre-registered kill criteria are laudable.** Defining quantitative thresholds (2pp for A3, rho 0.3 for COC, 2pp for SRPO stacking) before running experiments and honestly reporting failure against them is a practice the community should encourage. This raises the paper's scientific credibility relative to typical negative results reported post-hoc.\n\n2. **Root cause analysis is precise and educational.** The covariance degeneracy diagnosis -- K=10 samples in p=7 dimensions yielding p/n=0.7, forcing Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage to alpha=0.91 with effective rank 1.3 -- is a clean, quantitative explanation. This is the paper's strongest technical contribution: it concretely characterizes when multivariate CP becomes uninformative, providing a transferable lesson.\n\n3. **The per-perturbation analysis revealing augmentation-bounded correction is informative.** Showing that gains (where they exist) concentrate on perturbation types covered by synthetic augmentation, while uncovered types (layout, language) show no benefit, is a useful empirical finding about the limitations of inference-time correction trained on synthetic augmentations.\n\n4. **The experimental scope is reasonable for a negative-result study.** Eleven experiments on two benchmarks (LIBERO, LIBERO-Plus) with clearly defined ablations provide sufficient evidence to support the negative conclusions.\n\n5. **The paper addresses a genuine gap in the literature.** At the time of writing, no prior work had attempted to use structured per-DOF conformal statistics as learned correction features for VLA actions. Documenting that this does not work in the K=10 regime is useful.\n\n**Weaknesses**\n\n1. **The core negative result is largely predictable from first principles (Major).** With K=10 samples and p=7 dimensions, p/n=0.7 is firmly in the regime where sample covariance matrices are known to be unreliable. The Marchenko-Pastur law predicts ~30-40% relative error in eigenvalue estimation. Any statistician would predict that Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage would dominate, collapsing the covariance toward the identity and destroying the per-DOF structure that the entire method relies upon. The \"surprise\" value of this negative result is low. The paper acknowledges this in the paper framing (reviewer concern #1) and even identifies Ledoit-Wolf as a known risk in the MVP, yet proceeds with K=10 anyway without first establishing whether K=10 is sufficient. A more informative study would have swept K in {5, 10, 20, 50, 100} to find the threshold where multivariate CP becomes informative, turning a predictable negative result into actionable guidance.\n\n2. **No exploration of the positive regime diminishes contribution (Major).** The paper identifies covariance degeneracy as the root cause but does not test K>10. If K=20 or K=30 produces effective rank >3 and significant per-DOF benefits, the contribution shifts from \"multivariate CP does not help\" to \"multivariate CP requires K>N_threshold, and here is the cost-benefit curve.\" Without this, the paper answers a narrow question (does K=10 work?) rather than the broader one (when does multivariate CP help?). The title asks \"When Does Multivariate CP Help?\" but the paper only shows when it does not.\n\n3. **Only 10 references is insufficient for a venue like CoRL (Major).** Even for a negative-result paper, the related work section must comprehensively cover the space. Key omissions include: (a) \"Shifting Uncertainty to Critical Moments\" (arXiv:2603.18342) -- the most directly related work on per-DOF uncertainty for VLA actions; (b) TT-VLA (arXiv:2601.06748) -- test-time adaptation for VLAs; (c) PDF (arXiv:2604.18107) -- verifier-free test-time correction; (d) CRT (arXiv:2602.01158) -- observation-level corruption restoration; (e) SnapFlow (arXiv:2604.05656) -- relevant because 1-step flow matching could dramatically change K sample diversity; (f) Conformalized Teleoperation (RSS 2024) -- closest prior application of ACI to robot actions; (g) VLA-GSE, Cosmos-Policy, and other LIBERO-Plus baselines establishing the current SOTA landscape; (h) broader ensemble/MC-dropout uncertainty literature in robotics; (i) CP surveys establishing methodological context. Ten references signals either incomplete scholarship or a rushed submission.\n\n4. **No head-to-head comparison with external baselines (Major).** The paper discusses ReconVLA, A2C2, UPS, and SAFE in related work but does not experimentally compare against any of them. Even for a negative-result paper, showing that ACR-VLA underperforms or merely matches simpler baselines (e.g., ReconVLA's scalar CP selection) would strengthen the argument. Without these comparisons, the reader cannot determine whether the failure is specific to the multivariate CP approach or whether all inference-time CP-based corrections struggle similarly.\n\n5. **The three stated contributions are thin individually (Moderate).** Contribution 1 (negative result + covariance diagnostics) is the only substantial one. Contribution 2 (COC failure mode, rho=0.18) is a single correlation statistic that could be reported in a paragraph. Contribution 3 (augmentation-bounded correction) is an observation, not a contribution -- it follows trivially from the fact that learned correction generalizes poorly beyond training distribution. Packaging these as three contributions inflates the perceived scope.\n\n6. **COC signal failure mode is underexplored (Moderate).** The paper reports rho=0.18 but does not adequately diagnose why COC fails. Is it because the covariance degeneracy produces uninformative conformal regions (making the overlap check meaningless)? Is it because consecutive VLA predictions are too correlated? Is it because action chunk overlap structure is fundamentally uninformative about execution quality? Without disentangling these, the COC failure is reported but not explained.\n\n7. **Claim 3 testing is weak (Moderate).** The SRPO stacking experiment (+1.1pp, p=0.29) tests only one training-time method. The paper cannot claim \"inference-time conformal correction provides no orthogonal value\" from a single stacking experiment, especially when the base method (ACR-VLA alone) already shows only marginal improvement. The stacking failure may simply inherit the base method's failure rather than revealing anything about orthogonality.\n\n8. **The MVP positive result creates a confusing narrative (Minor).** The paper framing and memory.md show that the 3D point-reaching MVP passed convincingly (per-DOF 20-38% better than scalar). The full paper then shows the VLA-scale experiments fail. The paper needs to clearly explain why the MVP succeeded but the full pipeline failed -- presumably the MVP used oracle or K=10 with p=3 (p/n=0.3, well-conditioned), while the full pipeline uses K=10 with p=7 (p/n=0.7, degenerate). This transition should be the paper's central analytical thread.\n\n**Questions for Authors**\n\n1. Why was K not swept beyond 10? The covariance degeneracy diagnosis points directly to K as the bottleneck. Even a post-hoc experiment with K=20 or K=50 on a representative subset would dramatically strengthen the paper's conclusions.\n\n2. What is the effective rank of the sample covariance at K=20? If effective rank rises above 3-4, does the per-DOF advantage re-emerge? This is the single most important missing experiment.\n\n3. How does the MVP (p=3, K=10 → p/n=0.3) relate to the negative VLA results (p=7, K=10 → p/n=0.7)? Is there a critical p/n threshold between 0.3 and 0.7 where the method transitions from working to failing?\n\n4. Have you considered alternative covariance estimators beyond Ledoit-Wolf (e.g., graphical lasso, factor models, or assuming diagonal + low-rank structure) that might preserve per-DOF information even at K=10?\n\n5. For the COC signal (rho=0.18): is this measured on degenerate conformal regions? If the regions are essentially spherical (due to shrinkage collapsing to identity), the overlap check is uninformative by construction -- this should be stated explicitly.\n\n6. The full pipeline shows +3pp on LIBERO-Plus but -4.2pp on nominal LIBERO. Does this mean the CRM learns to \"over-correct\" already-good predictions? What is the per-perturbation breakdown for the nominal regression?\n\n7. What is the inference latency for K>10? Is K=50 prohibitively expensive, or could batched generation on modern GPUs make it feasible?\n\n**Missing Related Work or Baselines**\n\nThe following are absent from the 10-reference bibliography and represent significant gaps:\n\n- \"Shifting Uncertainty to Critical Moments\" (arXiv:2603.18342) -- per-DOF instability indicators for VLAs, the most directly comparable concurrent work\n- TT-VLA (arXiv:2601.06748) -- test-time VLA adaptation, relevant for the inference-time correction comparison\n- A2C2 (arXiv:2509.23224) -- the closest residual correction baseline; cited in text but not in bibliography and not experimentally compared\n- UPS (arXiv:2602.22474) -- CP for robot policy correction; cited in text but not experimentally compared\n- Conformalized Teleoperation (RSS 2024, arXiv:2406.07767) -- ACI applied to robot actions\n- Learnable Conformal Prediction (arXiv:2509.21955) -- context-aware CP scores achieving tighter intervals\n- Cosmos-Policy / WAM robustness study (arXiv:2603.22078) -- establishes LIBERO-Plus SOTA context\n- PDF (arXiv:2604.18107) -- test-time perturbation learning for VLAs\n- SnapFlow (arXiv:2604.05656) -- 1-step flow matching relevant to sample diversity\n- CRT (arXiv:2602.01158) -- orthogonal corruption restoration approach\n- DiU (arXiv:2605.00941) -- single-pass uncertainty for flow matching; the epistemic vs. aleatoric distinction is relevant even in a negative paper\n- Ensemble uncertainty and MC-dropout literature in robotics\n- CP survey/tutorial references (Angelopoulos & Bates 2023, etc.)\n\nMissing experimental baselines: ReconVLA (scalar CP selection on the same setup), A2C2 (residual correction without CP features), vanilla K-sample mean (simplest multi-sample aggregation).\n\n**Minor Issues**\n\n- The paper title asks \"When Does Multivariate Conformal Prediction Help?\" but only characterizes when it does not help (K=10, p=7). The title overpromises relative to the content.\n- 45D feature dimensionality: the paper framing describes 45D (7 std + 7 radii + ... ) but later project documents indicate 40D (action_dim=6, H_eff=5). This inconsistency should be resolved.\n- The -4.2pp nominal regression is concerning and underexplored. A method that hurts nominal performance while providing marginal OOD improvement has negative practical value in many deployment scenarios.\n- The framing positions this as \"systematic\" but 11 experiments on one VLA backbone (SmolVLA) and one benchmark family (LIBERO) is not systematic in the usual sense of the word.\n\n**Scores:**\n- Novelty: 1.5/4 -- The negative result is largely predictable from the p/n=0.7 regime. The covariance diagnostics are precise but the finding itself (small K + moderate p = degenerate covariance) is well-known in statistics. No exploration of the positive regime (K>10) limits the novelty of the failure characterization.\n- Significance: 1.5/4 -- Practitioners will take away \"do not use K=10 multivariate CP in 7D,\" which is useful but narrow. The absence of a K sweep means the paper cannot provide actionable guidance on what K is sufficient. The community would benefit more from a \"when it works vs. when it doesn't\" study than a pure \"it doesn't work at K=10\" report.\n- Clarity: 2.5/4 -- The pre-registered kill criteria and experimental structure are clear. The narrative transition from positive MVP to negative full-scale results needs better handling. Writing quality appears adequate based on available materials.\n- Related Work Coverage: 1/4 -- Ten references is far below the minimum for a CoRL submission. Multiple directly relevant works are omitted from both the bibliography and experimental comparisons. The positioning against the landscape is textually reasonable but formally incomplete.\n- Overall (novelty/related work perspective): 3/10 -- The paper documents a predictable negative result without exploring the boundary conditions that would make it genuinely informative. The thin bibliography and absent baselines compound the limited novelty. A negative-result paper at CoRL needs to either (a) refute a widely-held belief with surprising evidence, or (b) provide comprehensive characterization of failure modes with actionable guidance for practitioners. This paper does neither convincingly: the covariance degeneracy at K=10 is foreseeable, and the lack of a K sweep means practitioners learn only that K=10 is insufficient, not what K is sufficient.\n- Confidence: 4/5 -- I have strong familiarity with conformal prediction, multivariate statistics, and the VLA robustness literature. The covariance degeneracy analysis is straightforward to evaluate. Minor uncertainty about implementation details that could affect the results."}],"totalDurationMs":138876,"totalTokens":58155,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8192,"cache_read_input_tokens":46412,"output_tokens":3550,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8192},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3550,"cache_read_input_tokens":46412,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8192,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8192,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LC4i2ZbDghdW6HyQy2acaG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the roundtable:\n- Step 2: Cross-share (each reviewer reads the other's review)\n- Step 3: Convergence (identify agreements and disagreements)\n- Step 4: Synthesize into a final consolidated review report\n\nLet me now proceed with Steps 2-4 and then compile the final report for submission.\n\nLet me summarize the key points from both reviews:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments): 4/10**\nKey strengths: Pre-registered kill criteria, comprehensive ablation suite, mechanistic covariance diagnosis, feature importance \"smoking gun\"\n\n\nKey weaknesses: Only 3 seeds limits statistical confidence, conflates two distinct failure modes, never varies K across experiments, inconsistent baseline comparisons, COC analysis missing formal signal-to-noise assessment, SRPO stacking lacks proper controls, and the nominal degradation case remains underexplored.\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work): 3/10**\nKey strengths: Pre-registered kill criteria, precise root cause analysis, per-perturbation augmentation-bounded insight, reasonable experimental scope\nKey weaknesses: Results follow logically from first principles without exploring positive regimes where K exceeds 10, cites only 10 references, lacks external baseline comparisons, individual contributions feel thin, COC failure mode inadequately investigated, and Claim 3 testing is underdeveloped.\n\nThe MVP narrative is muddled. Looking at where both reviewers align: they both highlight the pre-registered kill criteria as solid, identify the missing K sweep as the most glaring omission, find the negative result somewhat unsurprising, note the sparse reference list, praise the covariance diagnostics, and flag nominal degradation as a concerning gap. Where they diverge is mainly on emphasis—R1 rates the experimental design slightly higher and values the methodology more, while R2 pushes back harder on novelty and predictability concerns.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9779,"cache_read_input_tokens":64983,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9779},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"685e604f-f9fc-4578-b777-ea9d186b1ac6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LC4i2ZbDghdW6HyQy2acaG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers have completed their independent assessments. Now conducting the roundtable convergence (Steps 2-4).\n\n**Step 2 Cross-Share Summary:**\n- R1 (Methods/Experiments): 4/10 — praises ablation design but flags insufficient statistical power (3 seeds) and the critical missing K sweep\n- R2 (Novelty/Related Work): 3/10 — finds the negative result predictable from p/n=0.7, only 10 references, no external baselines\n\n**Step 3 Convergence — Key Agreements:**\n1. Pre-registered kill criteria are exemplary\n2. Missing K sensitivity sweep is the single most critical gap\n3. Covariance diagnostics are the paper's strongest contribution\n4. 10 references far below CoRL standards\n5. Nominal degradation (-4.2pp) is underanalyzed\n6. The negative result has limited surprise value given known statistics of p/n=0.7\n\n**Step 3 Convergence — Key Disagreements:**\n- R1 values the experimental design more highly; R2 places more weight on the predictability of the outcome\n- R1 focuses on the two conflated failure modes (covariance degeneracy vs. CRM architecture); R2 focuses on the missing positive-regime exploration\n\nNow compiling and submitting the final report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9779,"cache_read_input_tokens":64983,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9779},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01d718a6-ac11-4c69-ac4b-36f76b2791b4","uuid":"39fa6aa1-d5ef-45cb-8c78-d78367542f44"}
